{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Rejoice greatly</b> — This is the beginning of the pericope, and the commentators are divided concerning it. Some say: this king is Messiah son of David. And some say: Messiah son of Joseph. And R. Moses the Kohen says: that he is Nehemiah the Tirshatha, and it is written in Ezra concerning him that he was governor in Judah; therefore he said: \"lowly and riding on a donkey,\" for if because of an animal — he did not mention a horse, for he was unable, because he is poor. And this is not correct, for he was a governor, and the governor did not seek bread from Israel, and every day many ate at his table — so how would he not have had a horse?! Furthermore: what is the meaning of \"your sons\" [against the Greeks,] and in the time of Nehemiah the Greeks did not rule over Jerusalem. And in my opinion: this king is Judah the Hasmonean, who was a warrior, as it is written: \"and I will make you like a sword of a mighty one,\" and his hand prevailed over the Greeks; and at his beginning he had neither wealth nor horses. And it is written in the words of our ancients a banned thing, that he was in poverty until he grew rich."], :book :zechariah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase 'דבר המנודה' (a banned thing) is unclear; rendered hyper-literally. The allusion to Judah Maccabee's poverty may reference a rabbinic tradition now difficult to identify."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Zecharia -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/zecharya"] ["1" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=25&perek=5&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:46:32.408029478Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}